WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:04.810
Good afternoon.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:10.850
We will go ahead and get started with the
Finding All Variants with ClinVar webinar.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.720
Thank you for coming.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.539
Please subscribe to our YouTube channel and
tell your friends.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:21.890
The purpose of this webinar obviously is to
show you guys how to find all variants with

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.070
ClinVar.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:31.490
The nice thing here, in either the NCBI homepage
or in ClinVar, you can search for either disease

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:37.120
or phenotypes, gene names, or HGVS.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:44.360
We're going to start by searching for phenotype
and here I've searched for cardiomyopathy.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.680
This is just from the NCBI homepage and I
will get lots of different hits with lots

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.530
of different things.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:01:01.140
For ClinVar there are tons of hits for asserted
phenotypic variants linked to cardiomyopathy.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:10.890
If I go in to ClinVar, and dig a little further
I will see that I can facet these 7075 hits

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.640
that I get over here on the left.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.590
These are all related to cardiomyopathy because
that is what I searched for.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.469
The first thing I could search for is what
phenotypic assertion is.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:25.630
Do they say this is pathogenic, a risk factor,
benign?

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:31.990
For the purposes of this demonstration we
are going to select the pathogenic one.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:38.369
Now we can see that the asserted clinical
significance is all pathogenic.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:47.929
Now, what ClinVar does is aggregate these
phenotypic assertions.

00:01:47.929 --> 00:01:53.990
We consider this to be more trustworthy when
multiple independent submitters have made

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.770
the same assertion.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:03.070
Here we are going to pick the ones that have
been made by multiple independent submitters.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:09.410
And then we might also be interested in what
the molecular consequence of his variants

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.410
are.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:15.099
We can see that we have some that are frameshifts,
missense, nonsense, and for the purposes of

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:19.489
this demonstration we'll choose the missense
one.

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:26.980
Now we see a whole bunch of genes here.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:34.950
We can also see in the display we get the
HGVS notation.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:41.129
Just to show you I can search by the HGVS
notation, and I don't need the gene name,

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:42.770
I can use the standard notation.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:49.750
And if I did that this would take me to a
specific ClinVar page.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:54.689
And this is what a specific ClinVar page looks
like and if we dig down I would like to also

00:02:54.689 --> 00:03:00.840
point out it is cross-referenced to the dbSNP
as well as the 1000 genomes browser and these

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:07.860
two stars show it has been submitted by multiple
submitters.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:15.080
Once again, we can also search by gene and
if we click on one of these gene symbols,

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:21.519
say, LMNA, it will take us to the Gene page
for that particular gene.

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:29.030
And I can see a whole bunch of the isoforms
down here in the sequence viewer.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:36.260
If I scroll down in the same page I can see
is that underneath the isoforms I get a whole

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:44.019
bunch of ClinVar variants and also other information
down here which is really cool and will be

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:46.689
the subject of another webinar.

00:03:46.689 --> 00:03:52.969
Anyway, I see the ClinVar variants and I say
am interested in one of the last exon, I can

00:03:52.969 --> 00:04:00.870
use the range tool tip to go ahead and zoom
in on the last part of the gene.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:09.469
Then I can mouse over one of those ClinVar
purple boxes in order to show the variants

00:04:09.469 --> 00:04:13.360
in that particular region of that particular
exon.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:20.240
There is another way I can do this and that
is by going to NCBI variation viewer, the

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:27.610
easiest way in my opinion to find the viewer
is to google NCBI variation viewer.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:35.660
Once again, we can enter a location, a gene
name or phenotype and we can find a particular

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:36.660
gene.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.160
Let's say we search for Lamin A, it will display
the gene symbol for lamin A, and if we click

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:46.460
on the blue arrow it will display the gene
for Lamin A.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:52.750
We can configure the display to show only
isoforms, but we can also select one of the

00:04:52.750 --> 00:05:00.220
last exons in this gene, zoom in, and then
we can see in the zoomed version the ClinVar

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:01.840
variants.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.340
By the way if you're interested in the variation
viewer here is a link to the full-length variation

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.340
viewer webinar.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:14.419
I will give you a couple of seconds to copy
that down.

00:05:14.419 --> 00:05:18.900
Also you can get there from the NCBI YouTube
channel.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:27.120
If we scroll down on the same page and variation
viewer we can filter these variants by a whole

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.610
bunch of different things.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:34.640
We can filter the only ones that are in ClinVar,
the ones that are in dbSNP or dbVar, that

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:42.500
is, either under or over 50 nucleotides, the
ones asserted as pathogenic or likely pathogenic

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:48.600
by ClinVar submitters, as well as the variant
type and molecular consequence as we discussed

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.240
earlier.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.880
When I go ahead and filter by these things
I can look at the pathogenic, the asserted

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.580
to be pathogenic variants, just in this particular
exon.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:06.680
The nice thing as well as I can see for each
of these particular variants how many publications

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:10.069
in PubMed support those particular assertions.

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:18.310
Back up to the top I can see the three pathogenic
variants in this exon by looking at the purple

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.380
color of those particular variants here.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:27.259
In fact, the different colors correspond to
different phenotypic assertions.

00:06:27.259 --> 00:06:34.860
I can also upload my own data into variation
viewer and that is covered more specifically

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.289
in that variation viewer webinar that I had
mentioned.

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:44.100
Finally I wanted to talk about batch download
for ClinVar.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:49.090
Really the easiest way in my opinion to do
that is to go to the FTP site.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:53.330
So if you go to ClinVar, which can be found
at this URL, www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/clinvar,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:59.780
or just by googling ClinVar, there is a link
that says go to the FTP site.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:09.159
On the FTP site it is trivial to get VCFs
of all of the ClinVar assertions or those

00:07:09.159 --> 00:07:14.069
assertions in XML, depending on what you prefer
to parse.

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:20.190
I would like to thank the people on this list,
formatted as a PubMed query, for help putting

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.199
together this presentation.

00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:28.570
I would be happy to take any questions from
the audience if there are any.

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:35.060
I should also mention that I am here with
Melissa Landrum, the group lead for the ClinVar

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:42.000
database, and she will also be able to answer
any questions should there be any I cannot

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.000
easily answer.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:51.289
This is an good question, is there a controlled
vocabulary for conditions?

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:53.530
If so, where and what is it?

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:56.480
If not how are condition names to be searched?

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.050
I believe Melissa will answer this more eloquently
to then I.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:04.580
So I am going to pass the microphone to her.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:05.580
Thank you, Ben.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:11.190
So in ClinVar submitters can provide their
condition to us in several ways.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:19.070
You can use an OMIM number, an HPO ID, an
Orphanet ID, a Concept ID from UMLS, so there

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.509
are several options.

00:08:20.509 --> 00:08:25.449
If your condition name is not represented
in any of the databases you can provide it

00:08:25.449 --> 00:08:27.550
to us as your own term.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:33.940
Once we receive all this information to ClinVar
we use the MedGen database at NCBI.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:39.430
MedGen is really an aggregator of condition
information from many different sources, including

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:46.070
the ones I mentioned, OMIM, HPO, Orphanet,
UMLS, and it also includes novel terms that

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:51.490
come in directly to ClinVar which are then
available to be incorporated into UMLS as

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:52.490
well.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:57.760
When you're searching in ClinVar you can search
by any of those IDs but you can also search

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:03.230
on just the term like the example of cardiomyopathy.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.550
All right, are there any other questions?

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.970
The slides will be available on the NCBI FTP
site.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:16.860
But there will be a convenient link on the
NCBI webinar's page, and again this video

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.630
should be available on YouTube in the next
week or two.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:25.200
If you liked the video and thought it was
informative, please tell your friends.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.680
With that, unless there any other questions
will go ahead and sign off.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.880
We hope you enjoyed this week's NCBI minute.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:41.820
We should either have a NCBI minute or a full-length
webinar every week for the rest of the summer.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.120
Have an excellent day.

